WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:05.560
Two inspection engines.

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:09.220
The same verdict on every comparable
frame.

3
00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:11.550
Both passed this defect.

4
00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:15.310
When an inspection engine on a production
line is

5
00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:18.880
replaced by a faster one, three questions
follow.

6
00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:24.990
On this coin-cell station, two cameras
inspect the top and bottom faces.

7
00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:28.850
The PLC keeps control: it latches the
verdict and grants

8
00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:32.540
the tray move, and the PC may write only 4
bits.

9
00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:38.130
The new engine changes 4 stages and keeps
the decision model.

10
00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:42.310
The main change replaces a
singular-value-decomposition illumination

11
00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:45.930
fit with regularized normal equations.

12
00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:51.170
In an offline benchmark, the decision path
drops from 4,542

13
00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:55.110
to 769 milliseconds per frame.

14
00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:00.140
The paper adds three things: latency seen
through the PLC,

15
00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:04.740
with every started cycle accounted for;
equivalence reported in layers,

16
00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:09.410
with margins; and engine agreement kept
apart from detection fitness.

17
00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:13.360
Equivalence is checked in 3 layers.

18
00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:18.140
On 276 development frames, 256 pairs are

19
00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:22.780
comparable, and the feature bounds hold
for only 3 of 11 features.

20
00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:26.080
Yet all 256 pairs keep the same verdict

21
00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:29.710
and the same driving feature, under both
decision models.

22
00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:34.050
9 pairs lie closer to a threshold than the
largest score change.

23
00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:37.630
Can a failure become a pass?

24
00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:42.630
Three failure classes were injected in
software at the top view: a missing region

25
00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:47.250
of interest, a decision exception, and a
simulated camera time-out.

26
00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:50.490
Each ended in a hold, with no verdict
written.

27
00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:55.080
With one run per class, this shows the
behaviour, not a failure rate.

28
00:01:55.650 --> 00:02:00.630
How long does the verdict take, seen from
the PC that polls the PLC?

29
00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:05.560
In 20 valid cycles of one session, from
conveyor stop to verdict latch:

30
00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:09.620
on the early-reject path, 22 to 54
milliseconds,

31
00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:13.550
which is the write and its latch, of the
order of the polling gap.

32
00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:16.940
On the bottom-view path, a median of 18
seconds,

33
00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:20.500
a quarter of the PLC cycle, dominated by
the robot transfer.

34
00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:24.380
This does not establish detection fitness,

35
00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:27.690
a failure rate, or the line effect of the
old engine.

36
00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:31.710
No pass was written in the measured line
runs, because the

37
00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:35.270
deployed model judged every bottom view as
defective.

38
00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:38.690
Scored afterwards on 186 labeled face

39
00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:43.090
acquisitions, with the bottom-view prior
matched to the current optics,

40
00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:47.150
both engines agreed on every verdict, yet
both passed 29

41
00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:50.690
of 48 confirmed-defect face acquisitions.

42
00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:53.460
Agreement does not establish detection
fitness.

